WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
A pH meter in zero point one zero

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.080
moles per litre hydrochloric acid reads one point

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.440
zero zero.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.480
In sodium hydroxide at the same concentration, it

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.200
reads thirteen point zero zero.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.120
Where do those numbers come from?

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.440
And sodium hydroxide makes hydroxide ions, not hydronium,

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.360
so how can it have a pH?

00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:25.606
pH is the negative log, base ten, of

00:00:25.647 --> 00:00:27.366
the hydronium concentration.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:31.366
Hydrochloric acid is strong, so every molecule gives

00:00:31.487 --> 00:00:34.646
its proton to water, and the hydronium concentration

00:00:34.807 --> 00:00:38.006
equals the acid's, zero point one zero, or

00:00:38.087 --> 00:00:39.766
ten to the minus one.

00:00:39.807 --> 00:00:41.446
The log of ten to the minus one

00:00:41.527 --> 00:00:44.246
is its power, minus one, and the minus

00:00:44.327 --> 00:00:47.526
sign makes it positive: pH one.

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:50.086
So each factor of ten is one pH

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:50.646
step.

00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:54.506
Nitric acid at zero point zero two five

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:57.626
moles per litre is strong, with one proton

00:00:57.707 --> 00:01:00.666
per molecule, so the hydronium is zero point

00:01:00.787 --> 00:01:03.306
zero two five, and the negative log gives

00:01:03.387 --> 00:01:04.986
one point six zero.

00:01:05.307 --> 00:01:06.906
Why two decimal places?

00:01:07.147 --> 00:01:09.226
The number before the point only shows the

00:01:09.387 --> 00:01:12.026
power of ten, so the precision sits after

00:01:12.107 --> 00:01:15.866
it: decimal places in pH equal significant figures

00:01:15.947 --> 00:01:18.746
in the hydronium concentration, two here.

00:01:19.967 --> 00:01:22.686
You can also run it backwards: the hydronium

00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:26.086
concentration is ten to the minus pH.

00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:29.006
A pH of three point four zero gives

00:01:29.087 --> 00:01:31.646
ten to the minus three point four zero,

00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:33.806
which is four point zero times ten to

00:01:33.887 --> 00:01:35.886
the minus four moles per litre.

00:01:36.447 --> 00:01:38.926
Two decimal places in the pH, so two

00:01:39.007 --> 00:01:40.686
significant figures in the answer.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:42.780
Now the base.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.820
In the last video, water gave protons to

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:50.140
itself, making equal amounts of hydronium and hydroxide.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.740
Its equilibrium constant leaves water out, so K

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.820
w equals hydronium times hydroxide: one point zero

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.300
times ten to the minus fourteen at twenty

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:01.260
five degrees.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.140
In pure water, each ion is ten to

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.340
the minus seven, pH seven.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.500
And it holds in every watery solution: add

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:13.020
hydroxide, and the equilibrium shifts back, using up

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.380
hydronium, until the product is ten to the

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.780
minus fourteen again.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.080
That value is only for twenty five degrees.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.200
Water splitting into ions absorbs heat, so warming

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.840
pushes the equilibrium right and K w grows.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.547
At thirty seven degrees pure water has a

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.720
pH of six point eight one; at sixty,

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.400
six point five one.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.440
It's still neutral, because its hydronium still equals

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:40.960
its hydroxide.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.760
So pH seven means neutral only at twenty

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:44.880
five degrees.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:47.620
Back to sodium hydroxide.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:51.060
It's strong, so the hydroxide is zero point

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.100
one zero, and the hydronium is ten to

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.980
the minus fourteen divided by that: ten to

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.380
the minus thirteen, pH thirteen.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:03.060
For a quicker route, use pOH, the negative

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.340
log of the hydroxide.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.220
Taking the negative log of K w gives

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:11.060
pH plus pOH equals fourteen at twenty five

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:11.780
degrees.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.380
So for a base, find the hydroxide, take

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:18.020
the pOH, and subtract from fourteen.

00:03:19.234 --> 00:03:22.833
Calcium hydroxide at zero point zero zero four

00:03:22.914 --> 00:03:24.273
zero moles per litre.

00:03:24.834 --> 00:03:28.353
Each formula unit releases two hydroxide ions, so

00:03:28.394 --> 00:03:31.473
the hydroxide is zero point zero zero eight

00:03:31.674 --> 00:03:32.353
zero.

00:03:32.434 --> 00:03:35.073
The pOH is two point one zero.

00:03:35.394 --> 00:03:38.273
Then the pH is fourteen minus two point

00:03:38.314 --> 00:03:41.313
one zero, eleven point nine zero.

00:03:41.394 --> 00:03:44.353
Stopping at the pOH is the classic slip:

00:03:44.434 --> 00:03:46.593
a base can't have a pH of two.

00:03:47.234 --> 00:03:50.433
Sulfuric acid is different: only its first hydrogen

00:03:50.514 --> 00:03:51.953
ion comes off completely.

00:03:52.594 --> 00:03:56.033
HSC questions usually say to assume both do,

00:03:56.114 --> 00:03:58.753
and only then is the hydrogen ion concentration

00:03:58.834 --> 00:04:00.033
twice the acid's.

00:04:01.234 --> 00:04:04.993
Adding water doesn't add or remove hydrogen ions,

00:04:05.074 --> 00:04:07.313
so the moles stay the same and only

00:04:07.354 --> 00:04:08.513
the volume grows.

00:04:08.994 --> 00:04:11.873
Twenty five millilitres of zero point two zero

00:04:11.994 --> 00:04:15.313
zero moles per litre hydrochloric acid holds zero

00:04:15.474 --> 00:04:18.433
point zero zero five zero zero moles.

00:04:18.674 --> 00:04:21.313
Made up to five hundred millilitres, that's zero

00:04:21.474 --> 00:04:24.513
point zero one zero zero moles per litre,

00:04:24.594 --> 00:04:27.073
so the pH is two point zero zero

00:04:27.194 --> 00:04:30.673
zero, three decimal places for three significant figures.

00:04:30.914 --> 00:04:34.193
Keep diluting and the pH creeps toward seven,

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:37.073
but never past it, because water supplies its

00:04:37.154 --> 00:04:38.273
own hydronium.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.060
Mixing an acid with a base is different,

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.380
because they react.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:47.260
Hydrogen ions and hydroxide ions make water until

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.660
one runs out, and whatever is left over

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:51.260
sets the pH.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:55.620
So neutralise first, then dilute: find both amounts

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:58.700
in moles, subtract, and divide the leftover by

00:04:58.780 --> 00:04:59.980
the total volume.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.320
Forty millilitres of zero point one five zero

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.680
moles per litre hydrochloric acid is mixed with

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:11.040
sixty millilitres of zero point zero eight zero

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.960
zero moles per litre barium hydroxide.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.120
The acid gives zero point zero zero six

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:19.440
zero zero moles of hydrogen ions.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.000
Barium hydroxide releases two hydroxide ions each, so

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.960
it gives zero point zero zero nine six

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:28.320
zero moles.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.880
So hydroxide is in excess.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:35.380
That leftover hydroxide spreads through all one hundred

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.900
millilitres, so it's zero point zero three six

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:40.900
zero moles per litre.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.860
The pOH is one point four four four,

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:46.660
so the pH is twelve point five five

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:47.620
six.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:50.580
Dividing by the acid's forty millilitres alone is

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:51.860
the classic slip.

00:05:53.067 --> 00:05:56.746
Remember: one pH unit is ten times.

00:05:56.987 --> 00:06:00.346
For a base, find the hydroxide first, then

00:06:00.747 --> 00:06:04.426
pH equals fourteen minus pOH, at twenty five

00:06:04.587 --> 00:06:05.146
degrees.

00:06:05.627 --> 00:06:09.226
And for mixtures, neutralise first, then dilute.

00:06:10.434 --> 00:06:11.353
Your turn.

00:06:11.634 --> 00:06:14.593
Twenty five millilitres of zero point one two

00:06:14.674 --> 00:06:17.713
zero moles per litre nitric acid is mixed

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:20.833
with thirty five millilitres of zero point zero

00:06:20.994 --> 00:06:24.673
five zero zero moles per litre potassium hydroxide,

00:06:24.754 --> 00:06:26.353
at twenty five degrees.

00:06:26.674 --> 00:06:28.873
Calculate the pH of the mixture.

00:06:29.154 --> 00:06:30.513
Pause here to answer.

00:06:40.934 --> 00:06:43.973
The acid gives zero point zero zero three

00:06:44.054 --> 00:06:47.013
zero zero moles of hydrogen ions, and the

00:06:47.214 --> 00:06:50.213
base zero point zero zero one seven five

00:06:50.374 --> 00:06:51.653
moles of hydroxide.

00:06:52.134 --> 00:06:55.253
Hydrogen ions are left over: zero point zero

00:06:55.414 --> 00:06:58.853
zero one two five moles in sixty millilitres.

00:06:58.934 --> 00:07:01.253
So the pH is one point six eight

00:07:01.334 --> 00:07:01.933
one.

00:07:02.214 --> 00:07:05.013
Using only the acid's volume gives one point

00:07:05.094 --> 00:07:06.213
three zero one.

00:07:07.434 --> 00:07:08.073
Last one.

00:07:08.314 --> 00:07:11.673
A potassium hydroxide solution has a pH of

00:07:11.754 --> 00:07:15.353
twelve point three zero at twenty five degrees.

00:07:15.634 --> 00:07:19.433
Calculate the hydroxide ion concentration in this solution.

00:07:19.594 --> 00:07:20.793
Pause here to answer.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:35.120
First the pOH: fourteen minus twelve point three

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.680
zero is one point seven zero.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.400
Then the hydroxide is ten to the minus

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.680
one point seven zero, zero point zero two

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:47.040
zero moles per litre, two significant figures from

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:48.440
two decimal places.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.880
Ten to the minus twelve point three zero

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:54.640
is the hydronium concentration, not the hydroxide.

00:07:55.867 --> 00:07:59.066
So for any strong acid or base: work

00:07:59.107 --> 00:08:02.186
in moles when solutions are diluted or mixed,

00:08:02.267 --> 00:08:04.906
divide by the final volume, then take the

00:08:05.067 --> 00:08:08.106
negative log, through pOH for a base.

00:08:08.347 --> 00:08:10.986
But check it's strong first: a weak acid

00:08:11.067 --> 00:08:14.586
only partly ionises, so its hydronium isn't its

00:08:14.667 --> 00:08:15.746
concentration.

00:08:16.027 --> 00:08:19.306
Next: practice questions, then weak acids.

00:08:20.534 --> 00:08:21.973
Here are the key notes.

00:08:22.214 --> 00:08:24.133
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:24.214 --> 00:08:25.013
to copy them.
